diagnosis_text:
Direct measurement contradicts every claim in the ticket. From `host_1_1_1_1` (10.1.1.2), all six data-center IPs in the 10.200.0.0/24 range — including `switch_server_access` (10.200.0.1), `dns_server` (10.200.0.2), and all four web servers (10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6) — respond to ICMP with zero packet loss and sub-6 ms RTT. HTTP GETs to all four web servers return valid HTML pages with exit code 0. The uplink the user blamed — `switch_dist_1_1` eth0 (172.16.0.7/31) to `router_core_1` eth2 (172.16.0.6/31) — is administratively and operationally UP, carries a live OSPF Full adjacency, and forwards traffic correctly. All five OSPF-speaking routers (`router_core_1`, `router_core_2`, `router_core_3`, `switch_dist_1_1`, `switch_dist_2_1`, `switch_server_access`) report Full adjacencies with no stuck or missing neighbors, and their routing tables carry correct OSPF-learned routes to every subnet including 10.200.0.0/24 and 10.1.1.0/24. The "Destination Net Unreachable" the user may have seen was a response to pinging an external IP (e.g., 8.8.8.8) that has no route in this isolated lab — not evidence of a data-center failure. There is no injected fault, no misconfiguration, and no hardware anomaly anywhere in the topology. The ticket is a false premise; the user should retry their application — the network is operating normally.
